In order to facilitate access to and the exercise of service activities, national authorities must assess existing authorisation schemes and their justification. This reporting obligation applies to the existence of such schemes, not their operational criteria or specific conditions. Chapter III – Freedom of Establishment for Providers 1. Authorisations  
Article 9  
Authorisation Schemes  
1. Member States may not condition access to or the exercise of a service activity upon an authorisation scheme unless specific conditions are met:  
(a) Article 10  
Conditions for Authorisation 1. Authorisation schemes must rely on criteria that prevent competent authorities from exercising their discretion arbitrarily. 2. The aforementioned criteria must be:  
(a) non-discriminatory;  
(b) justified by an overriding public interest;  
(c) proportionate to that objective;  
(d) clear and unambiguous;  
(e) objective;  
(f) published prior to application;  
(g) transparent and accessible. 3. Authorisation requirements for a new establishment must not replicate or duplicate those to which the provider is already subject in another Member State or in the same country. Liaison points referenced in Article 28(2), alongside the provider, must assist the competent authority by supplying any relevant information concerning such requirements. 4. Authorisation should permit the provider to operate across the national territory—through agencies, subsidiaries, branches, or offices—unless restricting the scope to a specific region is warranted by an overriding public interest.
